s. The 2028 race is an open contest because the incumbent president is term-limited, so neither party starts with an incumbency advantage. No public general-election polls, expert ratings, or market prices exist for this race yet, and both parties' nominees remain unsettled.\n\nIn the absence of polling, the state's partisan makeup drives the outlook. Democrats hold a clear structural edge in Hawaii, where Republican presidential candidates have not been competitive in recent cycles. The main uncertainties are the national climate in a race without an incumbent on the ballot and how each party's nominee shapes turnout. Until Hawaii-specific polling appears, the race sits in Likely Democratic territory, with a wide band of uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9253, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8734, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8703, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9295, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.874, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8784, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.877, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.874, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.8862, "margin": 13.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.878, "margin": 13.0}]}